{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Incogni for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2560,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone. Just letting you \nknow there’s new limited edition  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5240,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Merch at the Merch store. More \ninfo at the end of the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "May 8th, 2024. On the blazing surface of the \nSun, a collection of sunspots have been growing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14400,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for days, ignored by most of us but watched \nwith fascination and some confusion by solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21560,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "astronomers.These dark spots marked the presence \nof an invisible tangle of magnetic fields that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28640,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "held enormous magnetic energy—energy that \nwould be released in a series of eruptions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34520,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sending a blast of plasma and magnetic field \ndirectly at the Earth. Two days later this coronal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40840,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass ejection plowed into Earth’s own magnetic \nfield, causing auroras that were visible in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46320,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tropics. I was in upstate New York and the entire \nnorthern sky was ablaze, albeit dimly. And the Sun  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54320,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is only getting started. Solar activity is still \nincreasing in a sunspot cycle that is proving way  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60120,
    "dDurationMs": 11720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more intense than scientists predicted. \nJust how much stronger is it going to get?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The magnetic storm back in May was the \nstrongest experienced by the Earth since 1989,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76880,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we’re still at least a few months of the \npeak of activity in the current 11-year solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81880,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cycle. Which is weird, because this—the 25th cycle \nsince sunspot monitoring began—was supposed to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89240,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a particularly weak one. In fact, the strength of \nthe solar cycles had been decreasing for decades.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96320,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, the Sun’s activity for this cycle \nis rising far more rapidly than predicted,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101160,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards an imminent polarity reversal \nof the Sun’s entire magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what’s going on? Why does the Sun’s \nmagnetic field act so crazy and why is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112040,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it acting so unpredictably crazy right now \nand how much more crazy is it going to get?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117360,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To answer these excellent questions we’re going \nto have to ask some more basic questions like:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123080,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What is the source of the Sun’s magnetic \nfield? Why does solar activity change over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127640,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an 11 year cycle? What causes the \nmagnetic field to flip direction,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resetting the cycle? And how can we predict \nthe strength of a solar cycle in advance?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139040,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First though, a quick 101 on solar structure. \nThe Sun’s interior has three main parts—the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "core where hydrogen is fused into helium, a \nprocess that produces all of the Sun’s energy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148680,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then you have the radiative zone where fusion \npower is transported upwards by high-energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photons bouncing their way through the dense \nplasma. Finally you have the convection zone,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159440,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where energy is transported by flows of \nplasma carrying heat up to the surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164600,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The core and the radiative zone are fluids, \nbut they rotate like solid spheres, with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all latitudes completing a rotation in the same \namount of time. But the convective zone is much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 175960,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more sloshy and fluid-like. Over most of the top \n30% of the solar radius, the equatorial material  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183160,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotates once per 25 days, while the stuff at the \npoles takes a lot longer—34 days to rotate once."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190880,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Broadly speaking, the Sun’s magnetic field \nis generated in the same way as Earth’s—by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195360,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the dynamo effect. In the Earth, this \nhappens when circular currents in Earth’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "liquid metal outer core amplify small magnetic \nfields, which amplify the circular currents,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206520,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and through a positive feedback process \nbuild a self-sustaining global dipole field,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211600,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sort of like a giant bar magnet. \nWe did an episode on this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The processes behind the Sun’s magnetic field \nare less well established because it’s very  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219520,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hard to make measurements beneath the solar \nsurface. What I’m about to describe represents  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a set of current leading ideas, but this is \nsubject to modification as we learn more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230720,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Sun, it’s believed that interactions \nbetween the solid-like radiative zone and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236120,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sloshy convective zone lead to various swirly \ncurrents, for example through turbulence and/or  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241840,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the coriolis force. This kickstarts the \ndynamo effect just like in the Earth.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247160,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first, the magnetic field looks much like the \nEarth’s simple dipole field. There’s a north pole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a south pole, and great loops of magnetic \nforce forming concentric shells outside  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun and in the solar interior passing \nthrough the plasma flows of the dynamo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263240,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s magnetic field rotates at the \nsame rate as its mostly solid interior,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267960,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so that magnetic field is pretty \nstable. But that’s not the case for the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271760,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun. Its magnetic field is generated and \nsupported in the convective zone, where,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277000,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you recall, the equator rotates faster than \nthe poles. This differential rotation drags  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283080,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nicely-aligned dynamo generators and their \nmagnetic fields out of north-south alignment,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "twisting these field lines around the Sun. The \nresult is that the dipole field lines get slowly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transformed into a ring around the solar interior. \nThe technical terms for these two directions are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301760,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "poloidal—towards the poles for the original dipole \nfield—and toroidal—like a torus for the ring-like  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308400,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lines. This coiling of the poloidal into toroidal \nfield lines is known as the omega process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316440,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was step one in breaking the Sun’s magnetic \nfield. Now another cool bit of physics gets to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322840,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "work—magnetic buoyancy. So when you have a \ntube of magnetic flux in a conducting fluid  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329880,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also being in a gravitational field, the tube \nwill tend to rise to the surface. At a given depth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335600,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "beneath the surface of the Sun, the pressure has \nto be the same everywhere. In the magnetic flux  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341160,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tube, the magnetic field contributes part of that \npressure. That means the pressure of the plasma  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347600,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(the gas pressue) in that tube has to be lower \nin order to have the correct pressure for that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353400,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "depth. So we have lower pressure plasma that’s \nthe same temperature as its surroundings. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359440,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can only be true if the density of that plasma is \nalso lower. Therefore the magnetic flux tube rises  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards the surface. The stronger the magnetic \nfield, the stronger the magnetic buoyancy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371160,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so as the toroidal field winds itself up, \nmore and more field lines push to the surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378320,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And now those rising flux tubes experience yet \nanother influence—the Sun’s coriolis force. This  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385480,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is the same effect that generates hurricanes \non Earth. The speed of the Sun’s plasma varies  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 391080,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with latitude due to the differential \nrotation, but also due to the fact that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Sun is roughly spherical. The stuff at the \nequator has to travel a greater distance in one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399440,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotation to make it all the way around the sphere \ncompared to the poles. This leads to a further  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405960,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "twisting of the magnetic field lines, inducing \nkinks. These kinks can then protrude from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413200,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sun’s surface. This kinking of the toroidal \nmagnetic field is called the alpha process."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419960,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The strong magnetism in these kinks can \nshut down convection where they protrude  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424480,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the Sun’s surface. With heat no longer being \nefficiently carried to the surface in that region,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431280,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it cools down and so looks a lot darker than the \nsurrounding solar surface, leading to sunspots."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This whole story nicely explains some of the \nfeatures of sunspots. They tend to appear in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443320,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pairs with opposite polarity where the one \nmagnetic kink emerges from and reenters the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar surface. Sunspot pairs are often \nduplicated above and below the equator  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453840,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether the same toroidal field line \nbecomes kinked in both hemispheres."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now as our magnetic field gets wound up, \nit increases in strength. The relatively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464160,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weak poloidal field during the solar minimum \nbecomes a very strong toroidal field at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469640,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solar maximum. At the beginning of this process \nthe field lines pile up at mid-latitudes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that’s where sunspots first appear. As \nthe winding intensifies the toroidal field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 481360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "approaches the equator, and the sunspots follow, \nand the number of sunspots also increases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487680,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This migration of sunspots \nfrom high to low latitudes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490680,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their increase in number is seen \nin the so-called butterfly diagrams,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494960,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which show the number and location of \nsunspots over a series of solar cycles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499520,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The magnetic kinks emerging from the \nsolar surface have strengths several  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hundred times higher than the baseline \nsurface magnetic field. These kinks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510200,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are not very stable. There’s an enormous amount \nof energy stored in these tangled and twisted  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515440,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic flux tubes. They can release that energy \nwhen various tightly knotted or twisted flux tubes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522000,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reconnect into a more orderly configuration. The \npent-up energy is released in this reconnection,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the result is often an explosive ejection of \nplasma and magnetic field from the Sun’s surface:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a coronal mass ejection like the ones \nthat caused massive auroras back in May."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541360,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we’ve wound up our magnetic field \nand then tangled it into a horrible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545720,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chaotic mess. We know that this \ntangling happens in cycles,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550600,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but how does the Sun reverse this process? \nAs the toroidal field winds up to maximum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556320,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intensity and the coriolis force rotates these \nfield lines back into poloidal lines, we start  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get lots of north-south mini-loops. \nLoops beneath the surface of the sun"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569440,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "begin to connect to loops close to or above \nthe surface to form much larger magnetic loops.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576760,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because of the direction of the line twisting by \nthe coriolis force, these loops have polarities  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581760,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "opposite that of the original poloidal field. Over \ntime, the strength of these growing loops will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588920,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exceed that of the original field. At that point \nthe magnetic field flips direction. In essence,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595760,
    "dDurationMs": 7960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the little coriolis-induced kinks in the toroidal \nfield become the new flipped global dipole field.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603720,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the same time, that wound-up toroidal field \nis fragmented by all these magnetic reconnections  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609280,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it decays away. And so the whole process \nstarts again with a nice, clean dipole field. Ok,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617320,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now that we know how the solar cycle works. \nLet’s see if we can answer some of our questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623480,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know why the cycle happens and why it resets. \nBut why does this take eleven years? Well, because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631240,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s how long it takes. That’s the amount of \nwinding needed to turn the nice gentle dipole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "poloidal field into a tightly-wound and \nsufficiently unstable toroidal field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That 11 years is also an average. \nSometimes it takes a bit longer,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647520,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sometimes a bit shorter. There’s even evidence \nof double cycles in the past—two very short  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cycles masquerading as a single longer one. We \ncan actually look to the geological record to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657280,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "track solar cycles back 11,000 years. For example, \nBeryllium-10 is a long-lived radioactive isotope  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664880,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "formed when cosmic rays hit the atmosphere. \nStronger solar maxima mean more cosmic rays,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671400,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so by measuring its levels of beryllium-10 \nin Antarctic ice cores we can get both the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676960,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "duration and strength of ancient solar cycles. \nAnd it turns out solar cycles have lasted on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682760,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "average 11-ish years for at least as long as \nhuman civilization. There are fossil records  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688600,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that suggest that stability goes back \nas much as 700 million years. Now the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694520,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "average cycle length may be stable, but \nthe intensity of cycles fluctuates a lot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over the geological record, solar activity appears \nto rise and fall through several peaks and valleys  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706840,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that each last decades to centuries. The last deep \nvalley was the Maunder minimum in the latter half  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the 1600’s, right near the beginning of our \neffort to actually count sunspots year after year.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719040,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the depths of this minimum sunspot activity was \naround 1000 times lower than in the modern era."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725080,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most recent several cycles—since the \n1940s—have actually been of above average  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729040,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strengths, leading to what we call the \nmodern maximum. But over the past few  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734240,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cycles that maximum appears to have been \nwinding down—quite dramatically in fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740200,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leading some to wonder if we’re approaching \na new Maunder-like minimum. That is,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745280,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until the current cycle got started, when its \nhigh intensity defied all of our predictions.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752480,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how do we make these predictions in the first \nplace? And why are they so hard to get right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759680,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The strength of a given cycle seems to be \ncorrelated with a number of things. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764080,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strength of the current cycle plays some role \nin determining how strong the next cycle will be,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769360,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but there’s a lot of variation. A much \nbetter predictor for the strength of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "next solar maximum seems to be the strength of the \npoloidal or dipole field during the solar minimum.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779960,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that makes sense. After all, that poloidal \nfield is what gets wound up into a strong toroidal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785320,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field, leading to more kinks, more sunspots, etc. \netc. But the reconstruction of a new poloidal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791480,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field from the chaotic fields at the previous \nsolar maximum is much harder to predict because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796680,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it depends in detail on how well the kinks in the \ntoroidal field happen to line up and reconnect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802800,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "During cycle 24 there was speculation \nthat cycle intensity may continue to drop,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "leading to a weak cycle 25 and perhaps \ncontinuing the trend towards a new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812040,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "long-lasting period of low solar activity. But \nsolar experts didn’t place much stock in these  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818080,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predictions because at that stage we just \ndidn’t know. But as cycle 24 bottomed out,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824720,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the newly rebuilt poloidal field was stronger than \nexpected. In 2019, at the depths of this minimum,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833280,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "experts convened and produced this projection \nfor the number of sunspots and magnetic field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 838640,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "during cycle 25. It was supposed to be around \nthe same strength as cycle 24, relatively weak  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "compared to the modern maximum, but not continuing \nthe imagined fall towards a new Maunder minimum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this is what cycle 25 actually turned \nout to be—at least to date. A much steeper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858880,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rise in activity, indicating we’re in \nfor a stronger and probably shorter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863560,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cycle. Scientists have been updating their \nmodels based on ongoing solar activity. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869840,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The previous study estimated a cycle \npeak in 2025, but now the peak and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876600,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accompanying flip in the magnetic field \nlooks scheduled for later this year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what went wrong? Well, nothing really. \nCurrently we forecast the strength and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887200,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "length of a solar cycle based on various \nmeasurables—like sunspot numbers and field  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 892520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strengths and field properties at different \ntimes. But these are crude metrics for an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897200,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extraordinarily complex system of plasma and \nmagnetic field dynamics. Scientists are also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doing some incredibly sophisticated simulations \nof this complex system. But these simulations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909400,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can’t make detailed predictions—they’re primary \nvalue is in exploring the deep physics at work,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915120,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which can allow us to come up with better \nheuristics to use in our predictions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Solar cycle 25 is ramping up to be a good one. \nIt probably won’t be as strong as those in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 924920,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "peak of the modern maximum—although it’s too \nearly to be entirely sure about even that.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 930240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s likely that before this cycle is done \nwe’ll get some more spectacular solar activity,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935520,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and perhaps auroral activity with it. So keep \nyour eyes on the space weather forecasts and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941320,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maybe find a dark place to watch the sky \nnext time the Sun’s twisted field snaps,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946800,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sending blasts of magnetic plasma to \nbombard our terrestrial space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953280,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Incogni for supporting PBS. Incogni \nbelieves in the value of keeping our personal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958440,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "information private. But there are hundreds of \ncommercial databases and data brokers who will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964680,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "aggregate your personal information including \nyour name, address, and online activity. Icogni  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970240,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a service that will fight for you to have \nall your personal information removed from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975080,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data brokers as soon as possible. They will \nreach out to data brokers on your behalf,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980160,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "requesting your removal and fight any objections \nfor you. All you have to do is make an account  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 986760,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and enter the info you want removed. And you \ncan log into your account to check progress and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 992680,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "see which data brokers had your information. \nTo learn more about Icnogni just check out:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998320,
    "dDurationMs": 5663,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "https://incogni.com/spacetime \nand use the code spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1003983,
    "dDurationMs": 5337,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Twelve years ago on July 4th, 2012 the ATLAS \nand CMS collaborations announced the discovery  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1009320,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a new particle to a packed auditorium \nat CERN. It was revealed to be the Higgs  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014040,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Boson - the particle that makes the existence \nof matter and interactions as we know them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible. To celebrate the fireworks of that \ncollision we’ve designed our very own limited  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024160,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "edition UV glow print Higgs T-Shirt \u0026 Hoodie. \nBoth the t-shirt and hoodie are UV charged,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030560,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so after being out in the sun they’ll begin to \nglow. Because of the UV printing process, we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035440,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only are able to print a limited number. If you’d \nlike to receive your shirt before July 4th, then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042480,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’ll need to put your order in by June 23rd. \nCheck out pbsspacetime.com/shop  to see them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049960,
    "dDurationMs": 28880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a link the in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
